1,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8],[8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8,8]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The four test frames' counts for colours 2, 4, 3 and 1 come straight from the test input, but I did not run this program on any grid. It still has to be checked against the three demonstrations.\n\n**What the grid is made of.** Each input has two parts:\n- A left column of identical hollow rectangular frames, each 5 rows by 11 columns and each a single colour.\n- A scattering of small shapes on the right: plus signs, squares, dominoes, L-shapes, in various colours.\n\n**What changes.** All the small shapes disappear and are replaced by background. Each frame gets a few single dots of its own colour on its middle row. Reading the fact sheet's object list, the number of dots in a frame equals the number of small shapes (4-connected) of that frame's colour:\n- Pair 1:\n  - The colour-4 frame gets 2 dots (shapes at 4,18 and 8,15).\n  - The colour-8 frame gets 4 dots (1,20; 16,15; 19,18; 20,11).\n  - The colour-2 frame gets 1 dot.\n  - Colour-3 shapes have no frame, so they just vanish.\n- Pair 2: colour 1 gets 1 dot, colour 6 gets 3, colour 4 gets 2.\n- Pair 3:\n  - Colour 2 gets none, because there are no small 2-shapes.\n  - Colour 4 gets 2 dots and colour 1 gets 2.\n  - The 8-shapes have no frame and simply vanish.\n\n**Where the dots go.** They sit on the frame's middle row. The first is two columns inside the right edge (x1−2), and each next one is two further left, giving x = 9, 7, 5, 3 for a frame spanning x = 1..11.\n\n**Construction.**\n1. Find the frames: objects whose bounding-box border is entirely their colour and whose interior is entirely background.\n2. Erase every other object.\n3. For each frame, count the erased objects of its colour and stamp that many dots, stepping leftwards.\n\nFor the test input there are four frames, coloured 2, 4, 3 and 1. Each gets as many dots as there are small shapes of its colour. The 5- and 9-coloured shapes match no frame and are only erased.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    bg = background(g)\n    objs = objects(g, bg=bg)\n    frames, smalls = [], []\n    for o in objs:\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = o[\"box\"]\n        sub = g[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1]\n        h, w = sub.shape\n        is_frame = False\n        if h >= 3 and w >= 3:\n            border = np.concatenate([sub[0], sub[-1], sub[:, 0], sub[:, -1]])\n            inner = sub[1:-1, 1:-1]\n            if np.all(border == o[\"colour\"]) and np.all(inner == bg):\n                is_frame = True\n        (frames if is_frame else smalls).append(o)\n\n    out = g.copy()\n    for o in smalls:\n        for (y, x) in o[\"cells\"]:\n            out[y, x] = bg\n    emit(out, \"Erase all small shapes, leaving only the hollow frames.\")\n\n    for f in frames:\n        c = f[\"colour\"]\n        n = sum(1 for o in smalls if o[\"colour\"] == c)\n        if n == 0:\n            continue\n        y0, x0, y1, x1 = f[\"box\"]\n        my = (y0 + y1) // 2\n        for i in range(n):\n            x = x1 - 2 - 2 * i\n            if x <= x0:\n                break\n            out[my, x] = c\n        emit(out, f\"Frame of colour {c}: {n} small shapes of that colour -> {n} dots on its middle row from the right.\")\n\n    emit(out, \"Final grid.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"objects":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"ding the fact sheet's object list, the number of dots in a frame equals the number of small shapes (4-connected) of that frame's colour:"}],"template":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each frame, count the erased objects of its colour and stamp that many dots, stepping leftwards."}],"topology":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Find the frames: objects whose bounding-box border is entirely their colour and whose interior is entirely background."}],"count":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The four test frames' counts for colours 2, 4, 3 and 1 come straight from the test input, but I did not run this program on any grid."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Reading the fact sheet's object list, the number of dots in a frame equals the number of small shapes (4-connected) of that frame's colour:"},{"source":"final explanation","text":"For each frame, count the erased objects of its colour and stamp that many dots, stepping leftwards."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"3e9e7be270dd4329c46a230e5ce1b095836e7098441e749960ad02c3bd87f9f9","task_sha256":"bea4625bc272eacf8a7d3d2c0abab3b3859e73001c1471045ae264cc548943a9","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
